@startuml
' Figure - State Machine Diagram: account access state
!include _header.txt

[*] --> Active : account created

state Active {
  [*] --> SignedOut
  SignedOut --> Authenticating : present credentials
  Authenticating --> SignedOut : rejected / failures++
  Authenticating --> AwaitingSecondFactor : accepted [second factor enrolled]
  Authenticating --> SignedIn : accepted [no second factor]
  AwaitingSecondFactor --> SignedIn : factor valid
  AwaitingSecondFactor --> SignedOut : factor invalid
  SignedIn --> SignedOut : sign out / discard conversation
  SignedIn --> SignedOut : 30 minutes idle
}

Active --> Locked : failures reach 5
Locked --> Active : 15 minutes elapsed
Locked --> Active : released by administrator
Active --> Suspended : suspended by administrator
Suspended --> Active : restored by administrator
Suspended --> [*] : account retired

note right of Locked
  Lockout is time-bounded as well as
  administratively releasable, so a denial
  of service against one account cannot be
  made permanent by an attacker.
end note

note bottom of Active
  A second state machine in the system, shown
  because the lifecycle of ACCESS is as
  procedurally constrained as the lifecycle of
  EVIDENCE - and because a composite state is
  the natural way to express it.
end note
@enduml
